{"text": "The Cat.\n\nAll day, Omar thinks about the girl.\n\nWhy does she cry? Is she sick?\n\nThe shop is very busy today.\n\nThere is a small noise behind the bags. Omar hears it. But he has no time. \"It is the wind,\" he says.\n\nAt six o'clock, he closes the shop. He takes some of his mother's bread. He puts on his coat.\n\nThen he stops at the door. His heart is fast.\n\nOmar goes out into the snow. The snow is up to his knees.\n\nHe stands in front of the girl's door. He wants to go home. But he thinks of her sad face.\n\nHe rings the bell.\n\nA woman opens the door. She has the same yellow hat. The girl is behind her. Her eyes are red.\n\n\"Good evening,\" says Omar. \"My name is Omar. I make bread. Are you OK?\"\n\nHis words are slow. But the woman smiles. \"Hello, Omar. I am Anna. This is Freya.\"\n\n\"Do you have a cat?\" asks Freya.\n\n\"A cat? No,\" says Omar.\n\n\"But she sits on your window. Every morning!\" says Freya. \"She is grey and white. She drinks your milk. I call her Mimi.\"\n\nOmar thinks. The milk. The little holes in the snow.\n\n\"Every morning, I wave to Mimi,\" says Freya. \"And Mimi looks at me.\"\n\nSo the hello is for a cat. It is not for Omar.\n\nOmar feels a little funny. Then he laughs. Anna laughs, too.\n\n\"Today there is no Mimi,\" says Freya. \"There is a lot of snow. Where is she?\"\n\nOmar thinks again. The warm shop. The small noise behind the bags.\n\n\"Please, come with me,\" he says.\n\nThey go to the bread shop together. The shop is warm and quiet. It smells of bread.\n\nHe looks behind the bags of flour.\n\nThere is a grey and white cat. She sleeps. Her face is white with flour.\n\n\"Mimi!\" says Freya.\n\nMimi opens her eyes. She sees Freya. She goes to Freya and sits with her.\n\nOmar gives Mimi some milk in a bowl. Freya gives her a little bread. Anna makes tea.\n\n\"Do you like green tea?\" asks Omar.\n\n\"Yes, I do,\" says Anna.\n\nThey eat Omar's bread. They talk and laugh. Omar's words are slow. That is OK.\n\